WEBVTT

00:00:00.285 --> 00:00:04.370
A checkout touches the gateway,
authentication, orders, and shipping.

00:00:04.541 --> 00:00:09.820
In proxymock, start with an identifier you
already know and find the recorded calls

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:11.219
across those services.

00:00:11.785 --> 00:00:15.856
Open the demo recording in proxymock web
and go to Requests.

00:00:16.027 --> 00:00:20.132
The grid starts with all captured
transactions, so calls from different

00:00:20.132 --> 00:00:21.808
customers are mixed together.

00:00:22.352 --> 00:00:24.933
Open Filters, choose Full Text, and enter

00:00:24.933 --> 00:00:28.396
the demo customer's email with the contains
comparator.

00:00:28.567 --> 00:00:32.924
The same value can match a header, a
request body, or a response body.

00:00:33.485 --> 00:00:34.834
Switch to the Trace view.

00:00:35.005 --> 00:00:37.232
Follow one checkout from the gateway to

00:00:37.232 --> 00:00:39.859
auth and orders, then from orders to
shipping.

00:00:40.029 --> 00:00:41.992
The email found the matching traffic.

00:00:42.163 --> 00:00:45.405
The trace headers in this demo help show
which calls are nested.

00:00:45.952 --> 00:00:49.651
Select a hop to inspect its recorded
request and response.

00:00:49.822 --> 00:00:53.256
Here the checkout payload contains the
email you filtered on.

00:00:53.426 --> 00:00:57.106
The response contains the order ID and
delivery estimate.

00:00:57.652 --> 00:01:00.283
Use the same method with a customer ID,

00:01:00.283 --> 00:01:04.129
order ID, or another value carried through
your requests.
